===============================================================================
       T H E   S E P T I C   T A N K Z   ::   M U S I C   A R E A
        45 tracker modules from the '90s scene, the stuff we all leeched
===============================================================================

 WHAT ARE THESE FILES?
 ---------------------
 Before MP3s, music on BBSes came as "modules": a tiny song file holding its
 own instrument samples plus the notes to play them, so a 4-minute song fit
 in 100K. You played them back on your Sound Blaster or Gravis Ultrasound.

   .MOD   Amiga ProTracker format, 4 channels (later 6, 8 or more). The
          original. Mostly 1987-1993.
   .S3M   Scream Tracker 3 (Future Crew, 1994). The PC demoscene standard.
   .XM    FastTracker 2 (Triton, 1994). Instruments with envelopes.
   .IT    Impulse Tracker (Jeffrey Lim, 1995). The power user's choice.

 There's no MIDI in here on purpose. Almost every .MID file passed around
 on boards back then was somebody's unlicensed sequence of a pop song.
 Modules were different: original music the scene wrote and spread for free.

 HOW TO PLAY THEM TODAY
 ----------------------
   Windows:  OpenMPT (free, plays and edits everything), or foobar2000 /
             XMPlay.  Mac:  VLC plays all four formats.  Schism Tracker
             (Mac/Win/Linux) and MilkyTracker (Mac/Win, best for MOD/XM)
             let you watch the patterns scroll the way we used to.

 WHO MADE THEM
 -------------
 Every module comes from The Mod Archive (modarchive.org), which has kept
 the scene's music online since 1996. The board fetches each file from
 there when you download it. All music is the work of its composers,
 credited below. Shared here non-commercially, in the spirit it was
 released. Composers who want a track pulled: leave the SysOp a message.

